            "<strong>Kangaroo mother care (KMC)</strong> is the evidence-based standard for the small baby: <strong>continuous, prolonged skin-to-skin contact</strong> between the baby and the mother's (or another caregiver's) chest, <strong>exclusive breast-milk feeding</strong>, and early discharge home with close follow-up. It qualifies any baby who is <strong>clinically stable</strong> — breathing without support, not needing intravenous fluids, tolerating feeds by breast, cup or tube — <strong>regardless of weight or gestation</strong>; the WHO now recommends starting it immediately after birth even in babies who are not yet fully stable, in units able to monitor them. It keeps the baby warm by the mother's body heat, which adjusts to the baby's temperature; it halves infection, improves weight gain and breastfeeding, reduces apnoea, and lets the mother do the care she will be doing at home from the first day.",
            "The position is <strong>upright between the breasts, skin to skin, head turned to one side with the neck slightly extended</strong> (never flexed onto the chest, which obstructs the airway), limbs flexed, secured firmly with a cloth wrap or a KMC binder high enough to support the head, a cap, socks and a nappy on, and the mother's own clothes over both. It is continuous — day and night, including sleep, with the mother semi-reclined — and interrupted only for nappy changes and, where the mother must rest, by the father, grandmother or another relative. Feeds are on demand at the breast where the baby can suck, otherwise expressed milk by cup every two to three hours in the position; weight is checked daily; and the mother is taught what to watch for — apnoea or colour change, poor feeding, cold, jaundice, lethargy. Discharge is when the baby is gaining weight steadily (about 15 g/kg per day), maintaining temperature in KMC, feeding well and the mother is confident, with review within a day or two and then weekly until about 2,500 g; many units discharge from around 1,500–1,800 g with reliable follow-up.",
